Here's a script that does what you asked for, with the processing split into separate subroutines so each part is easy to test on its own.

```perl
#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use utf8;
use feature 'say';

# Check whether a string is a palindrome.
# Whitespace and punctuation are ignored, and the check is case-insensitive.
# Returns true if the cleaned string reads the same forwards and backwards.
sub is_palindrome {
    my ($text) = @_;

    # Remove everything that is not a letter or a digit.
    # \p{L} matches any kind of letter in any language, \p{N} any digit.
    my $clean = $text;
    $clean =~ s/[^\p{L}\p{N}]//g;

    # Downcase the whole thing so the comparison ignores case.
    $clean = lc($clean);

    my $len = length($clean);
    return 1 if $len == 0;   # an empty string is its own reverse

    # Compare characters from the two ends, moving inward.
    for my $i (0 .. $len / 2 - 1) {
        my $j = $len - 1 - $i;
        return 0 if substr($clean, $i, 1) ne substr($clean, $j, 1);
    }
    return 1;
}

# Read a line from the user and strip the trailing newline.
sub get_input {
    print "Enter a string to check (empty line to quit): ";
    my $line = <STDIN>;
    chomp $line;
    return $line;
}

# Print the result with the cleaned version shown, so the user can see
# what was actually compared.
sub report {
    my ($original, $is_pal) = @_;
    my $clean = $original;
    $clean =~ s/[^\p{L}\p{N}]//g;
    $clean = lc($clean);

    if ($is_pal) {
        say "\"$original\" is a palindrome.";
    } else {
        say "\"$original\" is not a palindrome.";
    }
    say "Normalized: $clean";
}

# Main loop: read, check, report, repeat.
sub main {
    while (1) {
        my $input = get_input();
        last if $input eq '';
        report($input, is_palindrome($input));
    }
    say "Goodbye.";
}

main();
```

Run it with `perl palindrome.pl`. It reads one line at a time and checks each, and you can quit by hitting Enter on an empty line.

A few things are worth knowing. The script uses `use utf8;`, which tells Perl to treat the source file as UTF-8 and to do string operations on bytes. That means it handles accented letters, Cyrillic, Chinese characters and emoji as characters, which is usually what people want. If you'd rather treat the input as plain bytes, drop that line and add `binmode STDOUT, ':utf8';` before printing. Without it, non-ASCII output may come out garbled on some terminals.

The regular expression `[^\p{L}\p{N}]` matches anything that isn't a letter or a digit, so it strips punctuation, spaces, symbols and everything else. If you'd rather keep letters from other scripts and strip only ASCII punctuation, use `[^\p{L}]` instead.

The loop runs to the middle of the string and compares each character with its mirror from the end. That's about as fast as a palindrome check gets.

The empty string is treated as a palindrome, and so is a single character. Say "a" and it says yes. Say "ab" and it says no. Type nothing and it quits.

If you'd rather not use a loop, a one-liner does the same check:

```perl
use strict; use warnings; use utf8;
my $s = <STDIN>;
chomp $s;
my $c = $s;
$c =~ s/[^\p{L}\p{N}]//g;
say lc($s) eq lc(reverse $c) ? "palindrome" : "not a palindrome";
```

The downside is that `reverse` builds a second copy of the string, which matters for very long input. The version in the main script doesn't.

If you have test cases in a file, say `t/palindrome.t`, the `is_palindrome` subroutine is set up to take them straight into a test suite.

Here's a program that does this. It reads the string and the pattern from the user, compiles the pattern, and counts the lines that match. It handles invalid regular expressions and empty input, and it shows the matches in an organized way.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.PatternSyntaxException;

public class LineMatcher {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter a text (press Enter after the last line): ");
        String text = scanner.nextLine();

        // Read lines until the user enters an empty line.
        // This lets you paste a whole block of text, including blank lines.
        while (!text.isEmpty()) {
            String line = scanner.nextLine();
            if (line.isEmpty()) {
                break;
            }
            text += "\n" + line;
        }

        System.out.print("Enter a regular expression: ");
        String patternInput = scanner.nextLine();

        if (patternInput.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Error: the regular expression cannot be empty.");
            scanner.close();
            return;
        }

        Pattern pattern;
        try {
            pattern = Pattern.compile(patternInput);
        } catch (PatternSyntaxException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: the regular expression is invalid.");
            System.out.println("Details: " + e.getMessage());
            scanner.close();
            return;
        }

        if (text.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("No lines to search.");
            scanner.close();
            return;
        }

        int count = 0;
        Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(text);

        // We count matches that start on a new line or at the very beginning.
        // This way a match that spills over line breaks is still counted once,
        // on the line where it starts.
        while (matcher.find()) {
            if (matcher.start() == 0 || text.charAt(matcher.start() - 1) == '\n') {
                count++;
            }
        }

        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Results:");
        System.out.println("Lines that match the pattern: " + count);

        // Show which lines matched, with their numbers, so the user can check.
        // Using split keeps blank lines intact, which split("\\n") would drop.
        String[] lines = text.split("\n", -1);
        for (int i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
            if (pattern.matcher(lines[i]).find()) {
                System.out.printf("  Line %d: %s%n", i + 1, lines[i]);
            }
        }

        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

The program treats the input as a block of text. You type or paste it, and it ends when you send an empty line. That's most of what's needed for a command-line tool. If you'd rather type the string on one line and the pattern on the next, with nothing extra, use `Scanner` twice in a row, and drop the loop. That version is shorter, and it's the one I'd use if nobody has to paste a long text into it.

The count uses `matcher.find()`, which finds the pattern anywhere on a line. If you need exact line matches instead, where the whole line has to equal the pattern, change `find()` to `matches()`.

A blank line in the input counts as a line. If your definition of a line is a non-empty sequence of characters, filter the array before you count it.

If the string is very long, the `split` on the second pass reads it into memory twice. For most inputs that's fine. If you'd rather not do that, count the matches on the first pass and write the matching lines to a list as you go, so the list holds only what matched.

The grouping in the output is plain text. If you'd rather show it as a table, with column headers and borders, `System.out.printf` is the one to use. If you'd rather not write the formatting by hand, JLine does it for you.

This program reads a CSV file, checks that it has at least one row, and then prints the data as a table with a header row and column names. It tries to read the file as UTF-8 and falls back to latin-1 if that fails, so it handles most text files without crashing. Each column is given a fixed width, set by the widest item in that column, including the header, and the rows are left aligned.

```python
import csv
import sys

def read_csv(path):
    """Read a CSV file and return (headers, rows)."""
    try:
        f = open(path, 'r', encoding='utf-8')
    except OSError as e:
        raise RuntimeError(f"Cannot open '{path}': {e}")

    if f.read(1) == '\ufeff':
        # UTF-8 BOM: skip it and keep reading
        f.seek(0)

    # latin-1 can read any byte, so it never raises UnicodeDecodeError
    try:
        reader = csv.reader(f, encoding='utf-8', errors='strict')
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        reader = csv.reader(f, encoding='latin-1', errors='replace')

    try:
        headers = next(reader)
    except StopIteration:
        raise ValueError(f"'{path}' is empty")

    rows = list(reader)
    f.close()
    return headers, rows


def format_table(headers, rows):
    """Return the table as a string."""
    if not headers:
        return ""

    widths = [len(h) for h in headers]
    for row in rows:
        for i, cell in enumerate(row):
            if i < len(widths):
                widths[i] = max(widths[i], len(cell))

    def cell(s):
        return str(s).ljust(widths[i])

    lines = []

    header_line = " | ".join(headers)
    lines.append(header_line)
    lines.append("-" * len(header_line))

    for row in rows:
        while len(row) < len(headers):
            row.append("")
        lines.append(" | ".join(cell(cell) for cell in row))

    return "\n".join(lines)


def main():
    if len(sys.argv) != 2:
        print("Usage: python table.py <file.csv>", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)

    path = sys.argv[1]

    try:
        headers, rows = read_csv(path)
    except (RuntimeError, ValueError) as e:
        print(f"Error: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)

    table = format_table(headers, rows)
    print(table)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
```

To run it:

```
python table.py data.csv
```

The program takes the file path as a command-line argument. If the file is missing, empty, or cannot be read, it prints a message to standard error and exits with a non-zero code, so a missing file does not produce a blank table.

One thing to know is that quoted fields containing commas are handled correctly by the CSV reader, but they are not parsed as numbers, so every cell is printed as text. If you need column types, such as dates or numbers, the standard library does not provide them, and a small amount of extra code would be needed to convert each cell.

This is a complete design and a working prototype. The prototype is one file you can open in a browser. The notes after it cover the parts you would build differently for real students, because the prototype does some things loosely.

The design

The user's journey runs like this. A parent or student lands on the page, types a subject or a grade into the search bar, and sees a list of tutors. Each tutor shows their photo, their qualifications, their experience, their hourly rate, and their average rating from reviews. They pick a tutor, choose a time on the calendar, and pay. After that they get a confirmation email and a reminder a day before the session. Tutors sign up separately, with their credentials checked by hand before they go live.

The search filters by subject, grade level, price, distance (if you add locations later) and availability. Every result shows how many open slots the tutor has. The profile page shows the same details plus a short bio, the subjects they teach, and a message button. The calendar lets users pick a date and a slot, and shows only times the tutor has marked as free. Payment uses Stripe, which handles cards and refunds and keeps the card numbers off your server. Reviews go up after a session ends, and only whoever booked that session can leave one. The messaging system sits inside the platform, so no one has to give out a phone number. It also holds the session notes, so both sides leave with a record of what was covered.

A parent can set up an account, add a child's name and grade, and book under that child. That child's own login would see the same sessions under their name.

